{
  "texts": [
    {
      "id": 0,
      "text": "Subtract ones, tens, hundreds, and thousands. This gives you 5,124."
    },
    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "You made a mistake in the tens column. 3 minus 1 is 2. The answer is 5,124."
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "You cannot swap numbers. You must regroup from the next column to get 10 ones."
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "You cannot just write 7. You need to regroup from the tens to get enough ones."
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "To get 10 ones, 1 hundred became 10 tens. Then 1 ten became 10 ones, leaving 9 tens."
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Yes, after regrouping, the tens place will have 9. This was shown in the example."
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "When you regroup from tens to ones, the tens digit goes down by 1."
    },
    {
      "id": 7,
      "text": "The tens digit became 10 after regrouping. Then 1 ten moved, leaving 9 tens."
    },
    {
      "id": 8,
      "text": "Regrouping is not swapping digits. It means changing 1 ten into 10 ones."
    },
    {
      "id": 9,
      "text": "Regrouping does not change the number's total value. It just changes how we write it."
    },
    {
      "id": 10,
      "text": "After regrouping, the hundreds digit is 5. So, 5 minus 0 equals 5."
    },
    {
      "id": 11,
      "text": "Correct! After 1 hundred was regrouped, 5 hundreds were left. So, 5 minus 0 equals 5."
    },
    {
      "id": 12,
      "text": "1 hundred was regrouped, so 6 hundreds became 5 hundreds. Then 5 minus 0 equals 5."
    },
    {
      "id": 13,
      "text": "The hundreds column is 5 minus 0 equals 5. The 4 is in the tens place."
    }
  ]
